WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.860
During DNA replication,

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.650
both strands of the double helix act as templates for the formation of new DNA 

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:09.510
molecules.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:15.550
Copying occurs at a localized region called the replication fork,

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:20.340
which is a Y-shaped structure where new DNA strands are synthesized 

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.650
by a multi-enzyme complex.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:24.890
Here,

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:28.280
the DNA to be copied, enters the complex from the left.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:31.700
One new strand is leaving at the top of frame,

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.350
and the other new strand is leaving at the bottom.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.250
The first step in DNA replication is the separation of the two strands by an enzyme 

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:40.860
called helicase.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.400
This spins the incoming DNA to unravel it, at 10,000 rpm in 

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.250
the case of bacterial systems.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:55.530
The separated strands are called 3' and 

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:55.880
5',

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.150
distinguished by the direction in which their component nucleotides join up.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:07.330
The 3' DNA strand,

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.100
also known as the leading strand,

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:14.160
is diverted to a DNA polymerase and is used as a continuous template for the 

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.150
synthesis of the first daughter DNA helix.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:21.310
The other half of the DNA double helix,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.080
known as the lagging strand,

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:27.960
has the opposite orientation and consequently requires a more complicated 

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.900
copying mechanism. As 

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:33.970
it emerges from the helicase,

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:38.950
the lagging strand is organized into sections called Okazaki fragments.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.400
These are then presented to a second DNA polymerase enzyme in the preferred 

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.910
5' to 3' orientation. 

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.250
These sections are then effectively synthesized backwards.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.840
When the copying is complete,

00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:00.850
the finished section is released and the next loop is drawn back for replication.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.630
Intricate as this mechanism appears, numerous components have been deliberately left 

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.580
out to avoid complete confusion.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:14.150
The exposed strands of single DNA are covered by protective binding proteins,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:15.860
and in some systems,

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.650
multiple Okazaki fragments may be present.

